# Editing This Ruleset

Rules for changing the ruleset itself. Keep it coherent and DRY.

## Only edit `core/` when developing the ruleset

`core/` is the shared, reusable ruleset — it governs agent behavior across
every project that adopts it. **Do not edit anything under `core/` unless the
task is specifically to develop or maintain the ruleset itself.** In a
downstream project, treat `core/` as read-only: put project-specific facts and
overrides in the project's `AGENTS.md` (see "Separation of concerns"), not by
mutating shared rules. The rest of this file applies **only** when that
condition is met — you are intentionally working on the ruleset.

## Domain organization

Rules live in **domain subfolders** under `core/`. Rules in subfolders are
**still active**. The domain folders **mirror the development process** —
`planning/`, `development/`, `testing/`, and `git/` map directly to the
process phases and stages in `process.mdc` (terms defined in
`definitions.mdc`). The current layout:

- `core/` (top level) — cross-cutting rules: `core.mdc` (editing/tooling),
  `definitions.mdc` (terminology), `process.mdc` (process map), `meta.mdc`
  (this file).
- `roles/` — how each agent role **behaves**: `roles/organizer.mdc`,
  `roles/doer.mdc`, `roles/auditor.mdc`, `roles/lone-agent.mdc`.
- `planning/` — the **Planning** phase: `planning/plans.mdc`.
- `development/` — the **Development** phase: `development/style.mdc`,
  `development/no-hacky-features.mdc`, `development/final-form-changes.mdc`,
  `development/parallel-subagents.mdc`, `development/independent-review.mdc`,
  `development/code-organization.mdc`.
- `testing/` — the **Testing** phase: `testing/unit-testing.mdc`,
  `testing/irl-testing.mdc`.
- `git/` — the **Git Stage**: `git/always-commit-work.mdc`,
  `git/git-branch-and-pr-workflow.mdc`, `git/pull-request.mdc`,
  `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`.
- `human-interface/` — human-facing communication:
  `human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`,
  `human-interface/answer-summary.mdc`.
- `documentation/` — documentation rules: `documentation/readme-sync.mdc`.
- `external-rules/` — external/third-party rules: `external-rules/ponytail.mdc`.

## Slot changes into their proper place

Any change to the ruleset should be slotted into its proper place in the
**development process**, and therefore into its proper **domain folder**.
When adding or changing a rule, first identify **which part of the process**
it belongs to (a phase, stage, or role), then put it in the **matching
domain**. Prefer an existing slot; create a new domain folder only when none
fits, and split a rule out only when it genuinely makes more sense on its own
(see `development/code-organization.mdc`).

## Separation of concerns

Keep these distinct — don't mix them in one place:

- **What a role IS** → `definitions.mdc` (terminology).
- **How a role BEHAVES** → `roles/*.mdc`.
- **Project-specific FACTS** → the project's `AGENTS.md`.
- **Generic BEHAVIOR** → rules here.

## Process enforcement is load-bearing

The ruleset's obligations, gates, and role duties are the substance of
`process.mdc` ("The process is mandatory"). An edit may **strengthen or
clarify** them freely; **weakening** one is a change the **developer asks
for explicitly** — never a byproduct of tidying, condensing,
deduplicating, or restructuring.

- **Modal wording is substance, not style.** "must", "never", and "only
  the developer" carry the obligation — don't soften them to "should",
  "prefer", or "consider" while improving tone or trimming length.
- **A pointer must carry the obligation.** Replacing restated text with a
  cross-reference is a deduplication only if the reader still lands on the
  same "must" (see "Anti-redundancy"). A pointer that loses it is a
  weakening.
- **Obligations survive a move.** When a rule is moved, renamed, split, or
  deleted, its gates and duties arrive intact at the new location — the
  sweep that repairs references checks that none were dropped along the
  way (see "Reference integrity on move").
- **New steps declare their skippability.** A rule that adds or changes a
  phase, stage, gate, or role duty states whether the step can be skipped
  and by whom, with the developer as the only party who can authorize a
  skip.

When a cleanup pass turns up enforcement wording that looks excessive or
redundant, **flag it for the developer** rather than trimming it.

## Anti-redundancy

**Single source of truth.** Cross-reference other rules instead of
restating them. When you move behavior, leave a **pointer**, not a
duplicate.

## Definitions-consistency workflow

When a term in `definitions.mdc` is added or changed, sweep the **whole
ruleset** and update everything to use it consistently.

## Cross-reference style

- Core siblings → bare filename: `process.mdc`.
- Subfolder files → path from `core/`: `roles/auditor.mdc`,
  `planning/plans.mdc`.

## Reference integrity on move

Whenever a rule file is **moved, renamed, or deleted**, re-check **all**
cross-references across the ruleset and update any that no longer resolve
— repoint them at the new location (in the correct style above), or
remove/repoint references to a deleted file. Leave **no dangling
references**.

## Editing mechanics

Follow `core.mdc` (PowerShell `;` not `&&`; `Move-Item` / `Rename-Item`
/ `Remove-Item` for file ops; specialized read/write/edit tools). Don't
restate its tables here.

## No testing needed

Editing this ruleset has **nothing executable to test**, so it **skips
the Testing phase**. "Verification" here means: `.mdc` files are
lint-clean, cross-references resolve (see "Reference integrity on
move"), and the change reads coherently — not running tests.

## AGENTS.md template

The repo root holds `AGENTS.template.md`, a scaffold for downstream
projects. It is deliberately **not** named `AGENTS.md` so it stays inert
here — Cursor auto-activates only a file literally named `AGENTS.md` (at
root and in subdirectories). **Do not** rename it to `AGENTS.md` in this
repo.

If work reaches the **user/IRL testing step** in a project that has **no
`AGENTS.md`**, **bug the developer** to create one from
`AGENTS.template.md` before proceeding — the testing facts can't be
followed if they were never captured. For the just-in-time prompt when
IRL requirements are missing, see `testing/irl-testing.mdc`.
